# This file ships as /usr/share/lintian/overrides/snitch (0644). It is the
# package's review record for the deviations it takes on purpose; lintian
# reads one tag name per line, and the comment above each line states the
# justification the review accepts it under (ci-debs design: "justified
# deviations only"). The names below are the tags lintian 2.144 (forky) and
# 2.129 (26.04) actually emit, renamed from the pre-split ones the first
# draft carried: the old combined dir-or-file-in-usr-local tag no longer
# exists in either archive's lintian, and an override naming a tag lintian
# does not know is itself an error (alien-tag) - the real triage run's first
# catch (ci-debs 6.4, recorded in docs/ci-debs.md).

# statically-linked-binary: both installed binaries (snitchd at
# /usr/local/sbin, snitch at /usr/bin) are built with CGO_ENABLED=0 and -trimpath,
# static by design. The package declares no Depends on any scripting runtime,
# C library or desktop environment (requirement 4.5) precisely because nothing
# in it links against one; a shared build would contradict the package's own
# metadata. A stripped static Go binary is the shipped product.
statically-linked-binary

# dir-in-usr-local + file-in-usr-local (the tag lintian 2.117+ split the old
# dir-or-file-in-usr-local into): the daemon sits at /usr/local/sbin/snitchd
# because both shipped units name that exact path in ExecStart, and that path
# is the frozen contract with the deployments that already run snitchd: the
# VM repo's pyinfra flow installs its copies of the same units and pinned
# binaries at the same place (requirement 4.2: "reachable at the path its
# shipped units name in ExecStart"). Moving to /usr/sbin would break every
# existing host at upgrade; /usr/local/sbin is where this product lives.
dir-in-usr-local
file-in-usr-local

# uses-no-compression-for-control-tarball + uses-no-compression-for-data-
# tarball: the deb is built with dpkg-deb --uniform-compression -Znone on
# purpose. It is the reproducibility recipe's core (requirement 7.2): with
# no compression step in the build at all, the compressor is removed from the
# artifact's byte identity - the alternative ("compress with whatever the
# builder's archive carries") had the two contract distros shipping liblzma
# 5.8.3 and 5.8.4, which produce different bytes for the same tree, so the
# deb's sha256 would depend on which distro packed it. dpkg-deb's member
# ordering and ar headers have been deterministic since 1.18.x, so the
# uncompressed archive is a function of the tree alone; nothing on the
# consuming side is affected - every dpkg reads an uncompressed member list
# the same way, and installation time is unchanged at this package's size.
uses-no-compression-for-control-tarball
uses-no-compression-for-data-tarball
